4. Theory
Tree in Data Structure
A Tree is a non-linear data structure used to represent data in a hierarchical form. It consists of a collection of nodes connected by edges.
Important Terminology
Term	Meaning
Node	Basic element of a tree
Root	Topmost node
Parent	Node having one or more children
Child	Node directly below a parent
Sibling	Nodes having the same parent
Leaf	Node having no children
Internal Node	Node having at least one child
Edge	Connection between two nodes
Degree	Number of children of a node
Depth	Number of edges from root to a node
Height	Longest path from a node to a leaf
Subtree	A smaller tree within a tree
Types of Trees:
1. Full Binary Tree
•A binary tree in which every node has  either 0 or 2 children.

2. Complete Binary Tree
A binary tree in which all levels are completely filled except possibly the last level, and the last level is filled from left to right.

3. Perfect Binary Tree
•A binary tree in which all internal nodes have two children and all leaf nodes are at the same level.

4. Skewed Binary Tree
•A binary tree in which each node has only one child.
